  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{5}^{3}4f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{3}4f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 4{\\int }_{5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -4{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
